     "body": "## What happens\n\nOn Windows, with agy on a magpie model, typing `agy` in PowerShell starts the real agy without the gateway or its key:\n\n```\nmodelProvider is set to \"gemini\" in settings.json, but the GEMINI_API_KEY environment variable is not set. ...\n```\n\nThe profile block (`# >>> magpie agy >>>`) is there and `magpie agy env` prints all three lines from Git Bash.\n\n## Cause\n\n`agyFunction`'s PowerShell body (`internal/agent/agy.go`) reads the variables with\n\n```powershell\nforeach ($l in @(& 'C:\\...\\magpie.exe' agy env)) { ... }\n```\n\n`magpie.exe` is a GUI-subsystem program (PE subsystem 2; `magpie.com` is 3). PowerShell doesn't wait for a GUI program it runs outside a pipeline, so `@(& magpie.exe agy env)` is empty, `$start.model` is unset, and the function falls through to `& $real @args` with no `GEMINI_API_KEY` / `GOOGLE_GEMINI_BASE_URL`. The comment above the function already says PowerShell doesn't wait for the GUI app; the call that reads `agy env` has the same problem.\n\nMeasured on Windows 11, magpie at v0.1.x (`.local\\bin\\magpie.exe`, Nov 6 build):\n\n| Call | PowerShell 7.6 | Windows PowerShell 5.1 |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `@(& magpie.exe agy env).Count` | 0 | 0 |\n| `@(& magpie.exe agy env \\| ForEach-Object { $_ }).Count` | 3 | 3 |\n| `@(& magpie.com agy env).Count` | 3 | — |\n\n## Fix\n\nRead `agy env` through a pipeline (`& prog agy env | ForEach-Object { ... }`), which PowerShell waits on for GUI and console programs alike, so it holds whether or not a `magpie.com` is beside the app.\n\nWorkaround until it ships: in the profile block, replace `magpie.exe` with `magpie.com`, then `. $PROFILE`.\n",
